## {#supply-demand-solutions-summary data-menu-title="Supply and demand: solutions" .smaller}

[Solutions]{.slide-title}

<hr>

(1) Supply passes through $(0,20)$ and $(100,70)$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
m &= \frac{70-20}{100-0} = 0.5 &\text{Compute the slope} \\
S(Q) &= 20 + 0.5Q &\text{Use the } y\text{-intercept } (0,20)
\end{aligned}
$$

(2) Demand passes through $(Q,P)=(40,70)$ and $(140,20)$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
m &= \frac{20-70}{140-40} = -0.5 &\text{Compute the slope} \\
P &= -0.5Q + c \Rightarrow 70 = -0.5(40) + c \Rightarrow c =90 &\text{Using } (40,70) \\
D(Q) &= 90 - 0.5Q &\text{Simplify}
\end{aligned}
$$

(4) Set $S(Q) = D(Q)$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
20 + 0.5Q &= 90 - 0.5Q &\text{Set supply equal to demand} \\
Q &= 70 &\text{Solve for } Q \\
P &= 20 + 0.5(70) = 55 &\text{Plug into either curve}
\end{aligned}
$$

At a price of \$55/lb, fishers supply exactly 70 (thousand lbs/week) of tuna and buyers demand exactly that same amount.

